import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> words = List.of("tree", "sky", "river", "stone", "tree", "moon",
                "sun", "lake", "river", "forest", "cloud");

        List<String> top = words.stream()
                .filter(w -> w.length() > 3)
                .distinct()
                .map(String::toUpperCase)
                .sorted()
                .limit(5)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println("Top: " + top);
        System.out.println("Original size: " + words.size());
    }
}

steps.body:
For numbers, switch to an `IntStream`, which has math methods built in. `mapToInt(Integer::intValue)` unboxes each `Integer` of a list to an `int`, and `Arrays.stream(int[])` streams an array directly.

```java
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

int total = list.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();
double avg = list.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).average().orElse(0.0);
int max = Arrays.stream(arr).max().getAsInt();
long big = list.stream().filter(x -> x > 50).count();   // count is a long
int squares = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 3).map(i -> i * i).sum();   // 14
```

`IntStream.range(0, 5)` gives 0 to 4 (end excluded), and `rangeClosed(1, 5)` gives 1 to 5. This is the one-line total that `forEach` could not build in the Lambdas lesson. Like any `int` total, `sum()` overflows silently past `Integer.MAX_VALUE`.

Some answers may not exist. `average()` and `max()` return an `OptionalDouble` or `OptionalInt`, and `findFirst()` returns an `Optional`. Use `orElse(fallback)` to supply a value for the empty case. `getAsDouble()` and `getAsInt()` read the value directly but throw `NoSuchElementException` on an empty stream, so call them only when the data cannot be empty.

Convert between the two worlds: `list.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray()` builds an `int[]`, and `Arrays.stream(arr).boxed().collect(Collectors.toList())` turns it back into a `List<Integer>`.

